{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1366162-was-creation-post-apocalyptic","id":1366162,"title":"Was Creation post-apocalyptic?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-07T17:02:16-08:00","id":1366162,"title":"Was Creation post-apocalyptic?","content":"I know that recently there was a thread about how postapocalyptic Creation is. I was hesitating whether to post it there. I decided not to, but I'd be absolutely fine with merging the theads.<br /> <br /> Encouraged by the general opinion on this board, I am just reading Exalted 1st ed corebook and I have to admit that it is indeed really solid. It has very distinctive mystery vibe straight from old Wolrd of Darkness which I find very appealing. However that's not what drew my attention the most.<br /> <br /> It's not hard to notice that in 1st edition together with anime tropes (enhanced in the 2nd edition) and bronze age tropes (enhanced, in my opinion, in the 3rd edition), there is a very strong post-apocalyptic feel reminiscent of Dying Earth or The Book of New Sun. Let me two quote two passages :<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> [M]ost cities are fed by land within a weeks\u2019 travel of their walls. The land between these oases of safety is sparsely inhabited. All settlements are built with an eye to defense, either walled or with a defensible central structure the community can retreat to if attacked. [...]<br /> <br /> Between these great cities and walled settlements lie great swaths of untamed wilderness, crossed by the trade routes of the Guild. Only armies and the massive convoys of the Guild can cross these areas with relative safety. Others who travel must rely on their wits or steel to protect them from the countless perils of the wilderness, from elementals and the fey to simple human robbers. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most of the Scavenger Lands consist of walled towns and cities and the farm lands immediately around them, surrounded by sparsely populated wilderness, connected by trade routes and a common heritage of immigration and restoration of the ruins that surround them. </div> </div> </div>The book consistently makes the impression that the apparent scarcity of dots on the map of Creation is not a negligence. Creation, so it seems, is huge, but really mostly <i>empty</i>. Or rather, filled with ruins or badlands full of inhuman weird danger. It's a full-fledged post-apo setting.<br /> <br /> I have the impression that 2nd edition was making this picture less and less consistent: while the Creation was still relatively empty, it was trying to fit in more and more cool stuff and, as a result, the setting felt generally weirder. At the same time 3rd edition tries actively to repopulate the world and make it full of interesting cultures and struggles. Generally, it also tries to make Creation more &quot;realistic&quot; and to steer away from the extremes of previous edition. I understand why it's desirable and yet, I feel really compelled with the bleak, desert-like vistas of 1st edition, where &quot;The Age of Sorrows&quot; was not a joke (actually, I love pure gonzo of Ink Monkeys, but these two are indeed very different settings).<br /> <br /> Inspired by that vision of the Creation, I started thinking of a (Dragon Blooded?) campaign set either in this alternative 1st ed Creation or, say, 10-15 years after the Great Contagion, when the Creation would still be genuinely postapocalyptic and still retain that feel (in order to avoid the clash of expectations with players). Has anyone tried something like this in this or previous editions? Either playing in such a &quot;shard&quot; or setting the game right after the Great Contagion? Do you feel that Creation used to be post-apo? And finally, do you agree with this depiction of 1st edition?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13416,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","user":"Lanic","date":". 02-07-2020, 08:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","name":"Lanic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13416_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"194"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-07T17:41:18-08:00","id":1366180,"title":"","content":"Yeah, absolutely, I don't think you're totally wrong. Exalted 1e was always doing a balancing act between sources which were:<br /> <br /> A) &quot;Post-apocalyptic*&quot; or &quot;frontier&quot; / exploration oriented - Dying Earth, New Sun, Zohthique, Hawkmoon, and lots of post-apoc-ish/frontier anime about at the time (Vampire Hunter D, Nausicaa, Trigun, Fist of the North Star, to some extent Xenogears or FFVI's World of Ruin, etcetc).<br /> <br /> (And of course, much of early-mid 1e was fairly oriented towards lots of exploration in fairly deserted ruins overgrown by jungle and the like. The original tagline of the game, which probably few people on these boards remember, was &quot;Before there was a World of Darkness, there was a world of Savage Adventure&quot;, right?)<br /> <br /> The sort of thing where you have art exploring the Sengoku Jidai or post-Roman Empire period or the Bronze Age collapse as a sort of tonal apocalypse are also part of this. <br /> <br /> and <br /> <br /> B) Sources which are interested in talking about fantasy in urbane, cosmopolitan, established locations which are not the simple kind of monster huntering, far removed from civilization fantasy gaming of much of stereotypical D&amp;D.<br /> <br /> This is the kind of where you get the incredibly rich (relative to most settings!), almost LeGuinian ambition towards cultural anthropology and diversity, or Exalted's explorations of Dynastic politics and intrigue which evoke Herbert's Dune and historical fiction like Robert Harris's Cicero Trilogy, or its attempts at weaving economic verisimilitude into world and narrative which evoke fantasy versions of &quot;hard edged&quot; steam/cyber/etc.punk explorations a la Gibson (I mention Gibson as recently reading him, but more in genre, Black Company is perhaps kind of a fantasy version of what this is gesturing at? Economically grounded genre about skilled, mercenary characters for hire who inevitably get caught up in events where they are required to be more than simple mercenaries.).<br /> <br /> There's no inherent complete conflict between these flavours (and each is arguably a bit of a set of disparate strands I've pulled together as a construct for my argument) but they are different currents which can be emphasized or de-emphasized and it does seem like a bit of a balancing act to me.<br /> <br /> Over time, I do think that there was probably a bit of a leaning in to (perhaps consciously or unconsciously) the sense that Exalted's selling point was having more emphasis on being a world full of politics and people.<br /> <br /> I do think that the 3e devs and core writers were and are certainly quite aware of this element of tonal shift and have tried to reintroduce some element of this simply by expanding the map so radically. But yeah, I think at the same time, recreating that same tone is really quite difficult, or takes a bit more. I love the feel of the world as presented in 1e, though I'm not saying that the perhaps &quot;busier&quot; feel of the world as 3e seems to depict it does not have its own charms.<br /> <br /> *in the sense of &quot;a major regression of a civilization, probably more 'advanced' than ours today&quot;, not necessarily totally coterminous with a sudden death apocalypse, or total collapse apocalypse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-08T09:27:02-08:00","id":1366340,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lanic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1366162-was-creation-post-apocalyptic#post1366162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the same time 3rd edition tries actively to repopulate the world and make it full of interesting cultures and struggles. Generally, it also tries to make Creation more &amp;quot;realistic&amp;quot; and to steer away from the extremes of previous edition. I understand why it's desirable and yet, I feel really compelled with the bleak, desert-like vistas of 1st edition</div> </div> </div> </div>These standards are not irreconcilable. The world has always been full of interesting cultures and struggles, at the same time as it has very often had a small enough population that you could have large sections in between settlements that were barely populated. It's hard to overstate just how dramatic the explosion in world population has been over the past century or so. The 20th century featured by far the most destructive war ever, and still saw world population rise from less than two billion to more than six billion. The United States alone, well in 1900 it possessed all the territory that it does today and had a smaller population than the modern UK.<br /> <br /> Walled cities, well, that's just practical in any era in which you can't be sure an army isn't going to come over the horizon and take what they please, or concerns over banditry.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-08T10:52:45-08:00","id":1366354,"title":"","content":"creation being like that is actually part of what I really like about the Exalted setting - sure, its a common fantasy trope to have ruins of some fallen elder race dotted around the landscape, but the repeated cataclysms and whatnot that creation has undergone makes for a much more interesting tapestry of stuff to throw at players","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malfeas F'Tagn - go check out my epic MLP/Exalted crossover &quot;The Scroll of Exalted ponies&quot; @ Fimfiction","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/588-webkilla","name":"webkilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar588_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-08T13:07:28-08:00","id":1366384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lanic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1366162-was-creation-post-apocalyptic#post1366162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> And finally, do you agree with this depiction of 1st edition?</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally I think that tone you're describing started to get diluted by Scavenger Sons which really started the trend of talking about two map dots in the same direction like they were neighbors but I've had fun playing in a game that was strictly the 1e core in terms of setting.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-09T09:36:14-08:00","id":1366575,"title":"","content":"I think it's common wisdom that Exalted is Post-Post-Apocalyptic. (Like the Legend of Zelda games.)<br /> <br /> The Divine Rebellion, The Usurpation, then the Great Contagion are all apocalyptic events which irrevocably shaped Creation, but the most recent of which happened over 750 years ago.<br /> <br /> Creation hasn't so much &quot;healed&quot; as it has &quot;scarred over&quot;.<br /> <br /> So it is filled with badlands and lost ruins and weird inhuman danger. But the space between the map dots isn't empty.<br /> <br /> There are minor settlements in thrall (culturally or literally) to the map dots. There is space for minor kingdoms, and cities comparable to the map dots, of your own devising. Those &quot;badlands&quot; might have a village like any other... or the locals might lick their lips whenever strangers roll into town. There are wild animals, and perfectly human dangers. A ghost town is just as likely to be the result of a mine drying up and the people moving on as it is to be a literal &quot;ghost&quot; town formed from dark magics.<br /> <br /> In 2E, even though city-states thousands of miles apart apparently had thriving trade relationships, this trade was facilitated by &quot;armies and the massive convoys of the Guild&quot;, and that's not <i>overly </i>dissimiliar to the Silk Road IRL.<br /> <br /> And looking at Bronze Age Real Life... we're not exactly talking massive population densities.<br /> <br /> ****<br /> <br /> That said, if you wanted to run a game within a decade or so of the Empress turning back the Fair Folk, I think that'd be awesome.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-09T12:20:21-08:00","id":1366605,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1366162-was-creation-post-apocalyptic?p=1366384#post1366384\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I think that tone you're describing started to get diluted by Scavenger Sons which really started the trend of talking about two map dots in the same direction like they were neighbors but I've had fun playing in a game that was strictly the 1e core in terms of setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair to Scavenger Sons, the map scale was much smaller when it was written, so those places really were relatively close to each other at the time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-09T12:35:57-08:00","id":1366611,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1366162-was-creation-post-apocalyptic?p=1366575#post1366575\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it's common wisdom that Exalted is Post-Post-Apocalyptic. (Like the Legend of Zelda games.)<br /> <br /> The Divine Rebellion, The Usurpation, then the Great Contagion are all apocalyptic events which irrevocably shaped Creation, but the most recent of which happened over 750 years ago.<br /> <br /> Creation hasn't so much &quot;healed&quot; as it has &quot;scarred over&quot;.<br /> <br /> So it is filled with badlands and lost ruins and weird inhuman danger. But the space between the map dots isn't empty.<br /> <br /> [...]<br /> And looking at Bronze Age Real Life... we're not exactly talking massive population densities.</div> </div> </div> </div>While &quot;glorious first age&quot; is a staple of fantasy, neither second nor third edition really seem use that trope to greater extent rather than to have cool ancient mysteries to uncover and ruins to penetrate. <br /> <br /> However, the description from first-edition really invokes (at least to me), images of classic postapocalyptic landscapes: settlements are few and isolated. Any travel is a huge enterprise. Note that according to the description, when you travel that land, quite soon after leaving Nexus you also leave farms surrounding it and then you basically go to wastelands. This is not really a landscape you would see while travelling through densely populated parts of Roman Empire. Essentially, you would be passing by farms, orchards, villas, taverns, milestones, your whole day. At no point would you go to genuine &quot;wasteland'.<br /> <br /> Maybe most posters here were looking at the world description in the third edition with first edition in the back of their heads. However, for me, reading the description of setting would invoke rather the landscapes of ancient civilisations. It seems to be depicted as a rather prosperous time, besides being the time of political unrest. However, it's not really &quot;the Age of Sorrows&quot;. Similarly, in the Scavenger Lands, people don't seem to actually Scavenge that much (which is described as an important activity in 1 ed).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","name":"Lanic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13416_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"194"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-09T19:03:11-08:00","id":1366679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\">But the space between the map dots isn't empty.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><br /> <br /> Mind, even empty space is something that can have different connotations to what some of us might expect.<br /> <br /> I know so little about the history of Africa, and yet what I have read seems to suggest some recurring patterns; rising and breaking apart empires, coastal mercantile cities, and most significant of all ethnic groups whose oral traditions maintain that they migrated to their most recently known long term territory a long time ago in response to some rival group (often backed up by decent archaeological evidence). <br /> <br /> I look at the interplay of these elements and the landscape of the continent and it creates a picture of people who are often on the margins of expanding empires who then just... pick up en masse and relocate elsewhere, because there's the space to do so.<br /> <br /> One finds a similar thing in history across Asia, although with somewhat more difficult environmental obstacles. Still, a significant portion of the history of Europe is the history of it being a verdant jewel lying at the destination of westward migrations in response to several factors (although the policies of Chinese dynasties features prominently).<br /> <br /> The thing that I'm getting at with all of this is that &quot;empty space&quot; can mean that a lot of the world can be open and inviting to large groups of people. Perhaps especially in this Time of Tumult. Unclaimed territory can provide one with freedom to select an area and start something entirely of their own there, or at least generate plot complications from running into other people in the process of doing so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lanic</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is not really a landscape you would see while travelling through densely populated parts of Roman Empire.</div> </div> </div> </div>Rome had political and commercial interests in the societies that existed beyond the margins of their Empire; Germans, Dacians, sub-Saharan groups, probably some Arab tribes, maybe the Bulgars are around at that point. There are a lot of things that could carry a Roman person far past the point where their network of settlements is well-established.<br /> <br /> At its height, Roman territory encompassed something that might be said to be roughly the size of modern Europe, and contained around 80 million people.<br /> <br /> This was a third of the world's entire population at the time.<br /> <br /> Keeping in mind that about another third is also concentrated in Han China.<br /> <br /> That's quite a lot of world out there to only contain 80 million people.</span></span></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-10T04:07:52-08:00","id":1366733,"title":"","content":"Beyond being post-apoc, one of the things that I find about Exalted's* version of post-apoc is that it is relatively... hopeful?<br /> <br /> A lot of post-apoc settings tend to emphasize the misery of people who are squatting in the aftermath of civilization collapse, the brutal authoritarianism and cruelties of political order (or disorders!) that crop up in the aftermath, and the fallen ruin of the world from its once greatness, ever diminished and diminishing until the fading sun blots out entirely. Creation has a fair bit of this, it's true.<br /> <br /> But I think there's also a lot of emphasis on: Exploring a world which is still pristine, vibrant and beautiful. The self-reliance, and the pioneering and positive mindset of the survivors rebuilding the world, and of vitality of the resilient cultures they've built**. (There's kind of clue is in the name: Scavenger Sons, emphasizing the heroism and agency of Second Age humanity). The 'Spirit of Adventure' that has been reborn in a world where there are still re-discoveries to be made, people to reconnect, new cultures that have not known each other and which can join in friendship.<br /> <br /> Kind of something I would tentatively call, &quot;Blue Sky Post-Apocalyptic&quot;. I feel that's something somewhat common in some of the particular JRPG and anime movie influences of the early Ex1e time** that use &quot;Lost Fallen Age of Glory&quot; aesthetics and setting elements, and perhaps is a bit less common in a lot of the pulp and S&amp;S (where perhaps the spirit of adventure tends to be tied more to early 20th century, a la Indiana Jones and similar two-fisted adventurers).<br /> <br /> (I guess also feels in common with the Age of Classical Greece when presented as a sort of sun-drenched age of new philosophy and ideas and Greek colonization following the Greek Dark Ages. Or maybe more tenuously the slightly dramatized end of &quot;The Long Pause&quot; that I saw in Moana when I caught on TV this Christmas, where this historical event was dramatized as a rebirth of the human spirit of adventure through the heroism of its culture heroes, Maui and Moana).<br /> <br /> It's a vibe that tends to be supportive of the idea that Solars who wander Creation and &quot;adventure&quot; - helping people across the world connect and fight off local dangers for just long enough to build something better for themselves - are actually doing something meaningfully heroic for the world and in the Unconquered Sun's vision. That wandering characters are not merely their shirking a proper duty - to seize power and leadership in a base and then try to reform political authority and fight unjust government.<br /> <br /> (Side note: There were a couple of other game settings that Exalted bore comparison to when it first came out; Earthdawn and Dawnforge, both of which had a similarly strong emphasis on the er... bright &quot;dawn&quot; of a new world, recovering after an apocalypse / after its first creation).<br /> <br /> Part of this is probably a bit of a niche differentiation when Exalted 1e came out - I think Geoff Grabowski clearly loved the idea of 'dying earth epic tragedy' that combines epic tragedy with a Dying Earth settings (so that's there!) and clearly they want to write in a way that an adult audience can respect and which touches on ideas they care about. But did they want to do another gameline *primarily* in tones of shadow and sepia, after the World of Darkness? Probably not so much.<br /> <br /> Again I kind of feel like 3e's clever writers are probably not unaware of this at all, and we'll see this coming through in the emphasis in the 3e version of the Scavenger Sons book, whose name I can't remember right now. (I feel like at some point this was literally called New Horizons or something like that, which would pretty damn on the nose, but this is possibly my delusion as I can't find anything like this online).<br /> <br /> *I originally wrote &quot;1e's version of...&quot; but I don't want to deepen and exaggeration any perception over what's in the books.<br /> <br /> **Without getting into negative &quot;Supporting the 'weakest' in society is bad and we should not do it!&quot;... unless you're a certain type of 1e Lunar, who is probably a villain or of ambiguous sympathy anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-10T04:51:45-08:00","id":1366739,"title":"","content":"Do you mean &quot;Across the Eight Directions&quot;?<br /> <br /> But yeah, I agree, it's a world in trouble but one in which you can drive out the bad guys, bring peace, build new civilisations, settle new lands, etc.<br /> <br /> (In 1st and 3rd ed. Some say not in 2nd ed, but let's not get into that.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-10T06:45:24-08:00","id":1366758,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1366162-was-creation-post-apocalyptic?p=1366733#post1366733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Kind of something I would tentatively call, &amp;quot;Blue Sky Post-Apocalyptic&amp;quot;. I feel that's something somewhat common in some of the particular JRPG and anime movie influences of the early Ex1e time** that use &amp;quot;Lost Fallen Age of Glory&amp;quot; aesthetics and setting elements, and perhaps is a bit less common in a lot of the pulp and S&amp;S (where perhaps the spirit of adventure tends to be tied more to early 20th century, a la Indiana Jones and similar two-fisted adventurers).</div> </div> </div> </div>Darn it, now I have to watch Nausicaa again some time soon.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's a vibe that tends to be supportive of the idea that Solars who wander Creation and &amp;quot;adventure&amp;quot; - helping people across the world connect and fight off local dangers for just long enough to build something better for themselves - are actually doing something meaningfully heroic for the world and in the Unconquered Sun's vision. That wandering characters are not merely their shirking a proper duty - to seize power and leadership in a base and then try to reform political authority and fight unjust government.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeesss, although I think part of the value of presenting such vibrant societies at every level is to make a point that the Solar who feels obligated to stick in one place finds plenty of opportunity for adventure and exploration as well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Part of this is probably a bit of a niche differentiation when Exalted 1e came out - I think Geoff Grabowski clearly loved the idea of 'dying earth epic tragedy' that combines epic tragedy with a Dying Earth settings (so that's there!) and clearly they want to write in a way that an adult audience can respect and which touches on ideas they care about. But did they want to do another gameline *primarily* in tones of shadow and sepia, after the World of Darkness? Probably not so much.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's a pretty succinct way to explain the tonal spectrum of that Edition.<br /> <br /> Part of it maybe being what can happen when one is utilizing a form of pulp media from an era of cynicism and pessimism (dying earth genre has to be indelibly connected to the fear of nuclear war, and the Black Company could not be more connected to Vietnam) from a perspective of enthusiasm for the material and during an era with more prosperity and generally looking forward to things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again I kind of feel like 3e's clever writers are probably not unaware of this at all, and we'll see this coming through in the emphasis in the 3e version of the Scavenger Sons book, whose name I can't remember right now. (I feel like at some point this was literally called New Horizons or something like that, which would pretty damn on the nose, but this is possibly my delusion as I can't find anything like this online).</div> </div> </div> </div>Fangs at the Gate and The Realm set a good precedent.<br /> <br /> I'd say those ones also do decent jobs of depicting the parts of the old world that collapsed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-10T07:38:22-08:00","id":1366773,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1366162-was-creation-post-apocalyptic?p=1366758#post1366758\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Darn it, now I have to watch Nausicaa again some time soon.</div> </div> </div> </div>I strongly recommend the manga as well. The movie ends somewhere around chapter 2 <i>of 7</i>, with some additional plot points from later chapters (like the god-warrior). The manga introduces a number of additional plot and setting elements that are highly relevant to Exalted, such as the rival empire of Dorok and old-world technology reminiscent of sorcerous workings or 2e's Craft: Genesis.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/97-the-mg","name":"The MG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar97_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2460"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}